<aside startOpen="true"><title>Stability theorem</title>

<p>Let <m>\frac{ dx }{ dt } = f(x)</m> be an autonomous differential equation.  Suppose <m>x(t)=x^*</m> is an equilibrium, i.e.,
<m>f(x^*)=0</m>.  Then</p>
<ul>
<li>if <m>f'(x^*)\lt 0</m>, the equilibrium <m>x(t)=x^*</m> is stable, and</li>
<li>if <m>f'(x^*)\gt 0</m>, the equilibrium <m>x(t)=x^*</m> is unstable.</li>
</ul>
</aside>
  
<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />

<p>Consider the autonomous differential equation <md>
  <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp= f(x)\;\;\;\text{where,}</mrow>
  <mrow>f(x) \amp= a x,</mrow>
</md>
and <m>a</m> is a nonzero constant parameter.</p>
<p>Below, move the two points on the graph to construct any function <m>f(x) = a x</m> that meets following conditions: </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the system has a single, unstable equilibrium.</li>
    <li>the value of <m>a</m> on your constructed graph will result in solutions <m>x(t)</m> that exhibit exponential growth. (Note, there are an infinite number of possible choices for <m>f(x)</m> but you only need to graph one.)</li>
  </ul>

<graph grid="true">
  <xLabel><m>x</m></xLabel>
  <yLabel><m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m></yLabel>
  <point name="dragPoint1" styleNumber="3" hide="$ans1.creditAchieved = 1">(4,4)
    <constrainToGrid />
    <attractTo threshold="3"><point>(0,0)</point></attractTo>
  </point>
  <point name="dragPoint2" styleNumber="4" hide="$ans1.creditAchieved = 1">(5,4)
    <constrainToGrid />
    
  </point>
    <line name="userLine" through="$dragPoint1 $dragPoint2" styleNumber="4" applyStyleToLabel="true">
      <label><m>f(x) = $userLine.slope x</m></label>
    </line>
</graph>
  
  <answer name="ans1">
    <award>
      <when>($dragPoint1 = (0,0) or $dragPoint2 = (0,0)) and $userLine.slope > 0</when>
    </award>
    <award name="notThroughOrigin" credit="1/2">
      <when>$userLine.yIntercept != 0 and $userLine.slope > 0</when>
    </award>
    <award name="notPositiveSlope" credit="1/2">
      <when>$userLine.yIntercept = 0 and $userLine.slope &lt;= 0</when>
    </award>
    <award name="bothPartsIncorrect" credit="0">
      <when>$userLine.yIntercept != 0 and $userLine.slope &lt;= 0</when>
    </award>
  </answer>

  <feedback condition="$notThroughOrigin">Double check the equation for <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>. It's given by the linear function <m>f(x) = ax</m>. This function represents a line with slope <m>m = a</m> and <m>y</m>-intercept <m>b = 0</m>. Does that match the line you've graphed?</feedback>
  
  <feedback condition="$notPositiveSlope"><p> Review the stability theorem introduced in the video. It tells us that if the derivative of <m>f</m> is positive at an equilibrium point, then the equilibrium is <em>unstable</em>.</p>
  <p>In our case, since the function <m>f</m> is linear, it's derivative is just <m>f'(x) = a</m>. Therefore, if we know that the equilibrium point <m>x_e = 0</m> is <em>unstable</em>, then the derivative <m>f'(x) = a</m> must have a positive value. Does this match the slope in the graph you have drawn for <m>f</m>?</p></feedback>

  <feedback condition="$bothPartsIncorrect"><p>Consider the function we are graphing, <m>f(x) = a x</m>. Isn't this just a line with slope <m>m = a</m> and <m>y-</m>intercept <m>b = 0</m>? That tells us the general shape of the graph.</p> 
    <p>How can we decide what to make the slope <m>a</m>? The stability theorem tells us that at an <em>unstable</em> equilibrium point, the derivative of the function is positive. Since the derivative of a function represents it's instantaneous slope (which in this case is the same throughout), this means that the slope, <m>a</m>, should be positive.</p>
  </feedback>

  
</exercise>
</problem>
</section>


